Methotrexate-Induced Acute Hematological Toxicity and Mucositis.

  ## 1. Case Scenario and Patient Narrative

### Initial Information
* Report Type: Initial Spontaneous Report
* Country of Origin: India
* Primary Source: Healthcare Professional (Rheumatologist / Clinical Pharmacologist)

### Patient Demographics
* Patient Initials: V.M.
* Age: 58 years
* Gender: Female
* Weight: 54 kg
* Relevant Medical History: Severe Rheumatoid Arthritis (3 years). Baseline renal function: Serum Creatinine 0.9 mg/dL. No prior history of bone marrow suppression.

### Suspected and Concomitant Drugs
* Suspected Drug: Methotrexate 15 mg orally once weekly (Indication: Rheumatoid Arthritis; Duration: Maintenance therapy for 1 year).
* Interacting Co-suspect Drug: NSAID - Naproxen 500 mg orally twice daily (Indication: Joint pain flare; Duration: Initiated 10 days prior to emergency admission without medical consultation).
* Concomitant Drug: Folic Acid 5 mg weekly (taken 24 hours after Methotrexate).

### Case Narrative and Timeline
* Year 1: Patient stable on Methotrexate 15 mg weekly + Folic Acid.
* Year 1 (Day 1): Patient initiated self-medication with daily Naproxen 500 mg twice daily due to an acute joint pain flare.
* Year 1 (Day 8): Patient developed severe oral ulcerations, difficulty swallowing, diffusely painful mucosal erythema, and epistaxis.
* Year 1 (Day 10): Patient presented to an emergency care facility in India with high-grade fever (39.0°C), severe purpura across arms and lower limbs, and prostration.
* Diagnostic Findings at Admission:
  * Complete Blood Count: Hemoglobin 7.2 g/dL, Platelets 22,000/µL, White Blood Cell (WBC) count 1,200/µL.
  * Absolute Neutrophil Count (ANC): 350/µL (Severe neutropenia).
  * Serum Creatinine: 1.9 mg/dL (Acute Kidney Injury secondary to NSAID therapy leading to reduced Methotrexate renal clearance).
  * Serum Methotrexate Level: Toxic concentration (0.8 µmol/L at 48 hours post-dose).
* Action Taken: Methotrexate and Naproxen were immediately and permanently discontinued. Patient was admitted to an isolation room in the ICU. Administered IV Calcium Leucovorin (Folinic Acid) rescue therapy, IV broad-spectrum antibiotics, and G-CSF 300 mcg daily.
* Outcome: By Day 12 post-discontinuation, mucosal ulcers healed, ANC recovered to 2,100/µL, platelet count rose to 110,000/µL, and serum creatinine returned to baseline (0.9 mg/dL).

